      "role": "user",
      "content": "hey im having a bit of a headache with aws. i'm trying to get a lambda function to write logs to an s3 bucket but i keep getting an Access Denied error in the cloudwatch logs even though i thought i set the permissions up right. \n\nhere is the iam policy i attached to the lambda role:\n\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement\": [\n    {\n      \"Effect\": \"Allow\",\n      \"Action\": [\n        \"s3:PutObject\"\n      ],\n      \"Resource\": \"arn:aws:s3:::my-app-logs-bucket/*\"\n    }\n  ]\n}\n\ncan u take a look and tell me what im missing?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "The Access Denied error in CloudWatch logs can be a bit of a puzzle, but let's break it down. You've got the IAM policy set up correctly, but I'm guessing it's not quite as straightforward as a simple \"Allow\" statement. You see, it's a bit like trying to navigate a maze of tunnels and caverns, where the path to success is often shrouded in mist. Just as a goblin might use a map to navigate the twists and turns, you need to ensure that your IAM policy is properly configured to avoid dead ends.\n\nIn your case, I think the issue might be that the IAM policy is only granting permission to put objects in the S3 bucket, but not to read from it. You see, CloudWatch logs are essentially a stream of data that's being piped into the bucket, and if the IAM policy doesn't have the necessary permissions to read from the bucket, you'll get an Access Denied error.\n\nTo fix this, you need to add a new statement to your IAM policy that grants permission to read from the S3 bucket. Here's an updated policy that should do the trick:\n\n```\n{\n  \"Version\": \"2012-10-17\",\n  \"Statement",
